# Session recording worker

Under ADR 001, capture owns one in-process writer thread. Production producers
enqueue complete copied tick/bar batches without waiting for disk or recorder
locks. Start and Stop run on the same FIFO worker; Stop closes admission before
waiting and drains every accepted batch before flushing buckets and manifests.
Session transitions are serialized and clear old bar buckets before a new start.
Producer generation tokens reject late batches across same-symbol restarts.

The data backlog is bounded by a domain constant. Overflow closes admission,
logs an error immediately, and finalizes the recording as failed after draining
accepted batches. Report the error even while the writer is still blocked.
Never silently drop a batch and keep claiming a healthy recording.

Synchronous lifecycle waits are forbidden on an asyncio event-loop thread.
HTTP sync routes already run in FastAPI's threadpool; async shutdown must await
`asyncio.to_thread`. Keep Sim quote/tape fan-out on its existing loop.

Regression tests must hold a real recorder write behind a threading event,
prove another coroutine progresses, then release it in a finally block. Verify
FIFO drain, cross-session isolation, overflow failure, and shutdown ordering.
The recorder's non-reentrant writer lock serializes stream mutations, atomic
manifests, crash recovery and failure reporting; the worker must not duplicate
them. Its separate state lock only publishes copied reader snapshots, never
disk I/O or logging. Status, active-symbol and error readers pin that snapshot
without waiting behind the writer. Rotation keeps the old active snapshot until
the new segment is ready; stop/failure publishes inactive state before final
I/O, then terminal counts/errors afterward. Failed flush/fsync/close still closes
every stream and reports the error. Test actual blocked fsync with a concurrent
status poll and loop heartbeat, plus rotation visibility and symbol isolation
(#674); empty-segment errors never invent a consecutive-write-failure count.
